---
// Case study: ProLog (draft), set as a short article in the site's own layout: the nav, a
// large heading, running text in the same second column as About and Services (plain bold
// subheads inside the text), and full-width images between. Light head and article, then
// the dark footer. Not linked from the homepage and kept out of
// search results (noindex, not in the sitemap) until Robin confirms the copy and the quote.
// Copy and open points: notes/cases/prolog.md.
//
// Images are built from sources in src/assets/cases/prolog/ (src/lib/shots.ts): the slide
// overview, logo sketches and page concepts are composed from the Figma files and the logo
// drafts; each has a phone version with fewer columns (the page concepts' from
// scripts/cases/case-phone-boards.py).
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import ProLogHero from "../../components/cases/ProLogHero.astro";
import CaseBoard from "../../components/cases/CaseBoard.astro";
import { shots } from "../../lib/shots";
import "../../styles/case.css";

const c = (name: string) => `cases/prolog/${name}.png`;

// The design in four steps, all cropped to the same desktop viewport (16:10, top of page).
const steps = [
  { src: c("prolog-step-1-wireframe"), alt: "Grey wireframe of the homepage", caption: "Wireframe: content and order first, before any visuals." },
  { src: c("prolog-step-2-light"), alt: "An early light homepage design with three figure cards", caption: "An early prototype, light and quiet." },
  { src: c("prolog-step-3-refined"), alt: "A refined light homepage with a diagram of how ProLog works", caption: "A later round, with a diagram of how ProLog works." },
  { src: c("prolog-step-4-dark"), alt: "The dark homepage design with an orange accent", caption: "The dark, precise direction the site launched with." },
];

const img = await shots([
  ...steps.map((s) => s.src),
  ...["prolog-logo-sketches", "prolog-logo-sketches-phone", "prolog-live-mockup"].map(c),
]);
const shot = (name: string) => img.get(c(name))!;
const FULL = "(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), calc(100vw - 64px)";
---

<Base
  noindex
  path="/cases/prolog"
  pageTitle="ProLog"
  pageDescription="A new website for ProLog, makers of offline log management in Germany: from the first briefing call to a Webflow site connected to their CRM."
>
  <header class="case-head">
    <Nav live={["about", "services"]} subpage />
    <div class="article__head">
      <p class="article__kicker">ProLog</p>
      <h1 class="article__title">A website that makes offline security easy to trust</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="article">
    <div class="article__media">
      <ProLogHero />
    </div>

    <div class="article__body">
      <p>
        ProLog, a software company in Pforzheim, makes log management that runs entirely offline, for public bodies,
        utilities and other organisations with strict rules on data. In 2026 I designed and built its new website, from
        the brief to launch.
      </p>
      <p>
        The old site had become the weakest part of ProLog's communication. It no longer did the product justice, and it
        wasn't up to scratch with what responsive design asks of a site today.
      </p>

      <h2>Understanding the problem</h2>
      <p>
        The project began with a brief, to understand what the site had to solve: who it is for, what brings them to it
        and what they need to know before they get in touch. Together with Martin and the team, we distilled a few clear messages and a focused
        structure for the content, so every topic has a job and the site is easy to find.
      </p>
      <p>
        One insight from the data and customer feedback shaped the rest: most customers didn't go looking for log
        management, they needed it because an audit asked for it. So the site starts there, and builds trust through
        precise language rather than big promises.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* Separate slides on the board colour (CaseBoard): seven to a row, five on phones. */}
    <CaseBoard
      folder="cases/prolog/boards/prolog-slides"
      rows={[0, 7, 14, 21, 28].map((n) => Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, k) => n + k))}
      phoneCols={5}
      even
      shadow="box"
      pad={3}
      gap={1.2}
      alt="Thirty-five slides from the kickoff presentation laid out in a grid"
      caption="The kickoff presentation: brief, strategy, logo and first drafts."
    />

    <div class="article__body">
      <h2>A mark for every size</h2>
      <p>
        The old logo was an all-caps wordmark. It read as one standalone term, so the “log” in the name got lost, and it
        offered nothing to use where space is tight. The exploration covered many variations on protection, logs and
        layers of security, and settled on a padlock built from lines of a log. The icon was drawn to stay clear at the
        smallest sizes, down to a favicon, and the logo now comes as a set: wordmark, lockup and icon.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article__media">
      <picture>
        <source media="(max-width: 767px)" srcset={shot("prolog-logo-sketches-phone").srcset} sizes={FULL} />
        <img
          class="article__image"
          src={shot("prolog-logo-sketches").src}
          srcset={shot("prolog-logo-sketches").srcset}
          sizes={FULL}
          width={shot("prolog-logo-sketches").width}
          height={shot("prolog-logo-sketches").height}
          alt="Pencil sketches of logo ideas: log lines, padlocks, stacked layers, keys and a shield"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Early logo drafts.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="article__body">
      <h2>Shaped in iterations</h2>
      <p>
        Wireframes came first, to settle what the site needed to convey: how much detail customers need to make an
        informed decision, and how much a security company should keep to itself when explaining its processes online.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once that was agreed and refined, the design grew through prototypes and feedback until the direction was clear:
        dark, calm and precise. The hero settled on an abstract picture of the company's work: a quiet stream of log
        lines like the data ProLog collects, pouring in continuously, obscured but open to inspection.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article__media gallery" data-gallery>
      <div class="gallery__track" tabindex="0" aria-label="The design in four steps">
        {
          steps.map((s, i) => (
            <div class="gallery__slide" data-caption={s.caption} role="group" aria-label={`${i + 1} of ${steps.length}`}>
              <div class="gallery__frame">
                <img
                  src={img.get(s.src)!.src}
                  srcset={img.get(s.src)!.srcset}
                  sizes="(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 80px), 1000px"
                  alt={s.alt}
                  width={img.get(s.src)!.width}
                  height={img.get(s.src)!.height}
                  decoding="async"
                />
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="gallery__bar">
        <figcaption class="gallery__caption" aria-live="polite">{steps[0].caption}</figcaption>
        <div class="gallery__nav">
          <span class="gallery__count"><span data-gallery-index>1</span> / {steps.length}</span>
          <button type="button" class="gallery__button" data-gallery-prev aria-label="Previous image">
            <span class="gallery__arrow gallery__arrow--prev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="gallery__button" data-gallery-next aria-label="Next image">
            <span class="gallery__arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="article__body">
      <h2>Ready to grow</h2>
      <p>
        The homepage was only the start. Alongside it we developed the pages that come next, from articles to sector
        pages, as a joint plan for how the site can and should grow: to answer customers' needs and pain points, to make
        information easier to find and come back to, and to present ProLog's services in the depth they deserve.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* Three tall pages cut from the top, running off the bottom of the board, the middle one
        lower; two on phones. The project drawer shows the same board (projects.ts). */}
    <CaseBoard
      folder="cases/prolog/boards/prolog-page-concepts"
      phoneRows={[[0, 2]]}
      crop={2}
      phoneCrop={1.5}
      drops={[0, 3.75, 0]}
      pad={6.9}
      gap={3.1}
      alt="Three page designs side by side: a blog article, a page for public administration and an audit page"
      caption="Concepts for an article, a sector page and an audit page."
    />

    <div class="article__body">
      <h2>Connected from day one</h2>
      <p>
        It was important to tie the site into the systems ProLog already runs, for the best possible workflow for
        Martin and his team. It launched connected to Odoo, so enquiries, demo bookings and security reports arrive
        where they already work, with nothing copied over by hand.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="article__media">
      <img
        class="article__image"
        src={shot("prolog-live-mockup").src}
        srcset={shot("prolog-live-mockup").srcset}
        sizes={FULL}
        width={shot("prolog-live-mockup").width}
        height={shot("prolog-live-mockup").height}
        alt="The ProLog homepage on a laptop and on a phone held in a hand"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The launched site, optimised for every viewport, including the mobile screens where many visitors first see it.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <!-- Martin's words, translated from his German message (Robin, 2026-11-15); to confirm with him before launch. -->
    <blockquote class="article__quote">
      <p>“I'm still thrilled by how confidently Robin built our new site, and how good it turned out. Really impressed!”</p>
      <footer>Martin Schlicksupp, CEO, ProLog AG</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <!-- Links out, in the style of the project drawer's. -->
    <div class="article__links">
      <a class="article__link" href="https://www.prolog.ag" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-umami-event="outbound" data-umami-event-project="prolog" data-umami-event-link="website" data-umami-event-from="case">
        Visit prolog.ag
        <span class="article__link-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<script>
  import "../../scripts/case-gallery";
</script>

